WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.900
Prisma Client helps avoid manual SQL construction for ordinary model operations, but a running Node.js application still receives untyped runtime data.

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:20.600
Request bodies, query strings, route parameters, webhook payloads, and queue messages arrive as data from outside the TypeScript type system.

3
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:34.220
A public API should not let callers submit arbitrary nested Prisma query objects and then pass those objects into where, data, orderBy, include, select, pagination, filtering, or relation operations.

4
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.900
Validation should be tied to business meaning.

5
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.720
If an endpoint supports a status filter, define the allowed statuses.

6
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:47.580
If it supports sorting, map user-facing sort choices to known fields and directions.

7
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.240
If it supports pagination, validate page size and offsets.

8
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.660
If it supports date ranges, enforce format and range rules.

9
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.040
Server-side validation is required; client-side validation is only a usability aid.

10
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:13.340
Validation should cover fields, types, lengths, ranges, enum values, date ranges, pagination values, supported filters, and relation behavior.

11
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.700
The safer pattern is to accept a small business-level request and map it to a controlled Prisma query.

12
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.160
That mapping separates user-provided values from application-controlled query structure.

13
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.500
Validation supports security, but it does not replace authorization.

14
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.280
A valid record ID still needs an object-level access check.

15
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.060
A valid tenant identifier should not be trusted merely because the client supplied it.

16
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:47.320
Query safety means the application controls the shape, validates the values, and enforces permission before returning or changing data.
